# The exact fork releases a MeshBench build fetches, pinned by tag.
#
# Sourced by .github/workflows/package.yml, and checked against
# internal/app/resource/toolchaincatalogue.go by toolchainpins_test.go, so
# the emulator CI puts in a bundle and the emulator a first run downloads onto
# somebody's own machine can never be two different builds.
#
# The pipeline used to ask both forks for /latest. Anybody re-cutting a fork
# release then changed what every MeshBench build bundled with no commit here
# to show for it, which is how a QEMU release that renamed its assets and
# dropped every platform but Linux reached three platforms' bundles under a
# green pipeline. Moving a pin is now an edit with a diff.
#
# The rule a pin is held to:
#
#   - it names a tag, never "latest" and never a branch
#   - the tag's release is built from a named commit on the fork's
#     meshbench-main branch, and that commit is recorded here. Where the tag's
#     own name carries it, as Renode's does, that is enough; where it cannot,
#     it is written beside the pin. QEMU's release workflow fires on "v*" tags
#     alone, so a date-and-sha name never reaches it, and the commit on a line
#     of its own is worth more than a name nobody can publish
#   - asset names are exact, because "the release exists and carries something
#     else" is only sayable when we know what was expected
#   - an empty asset is a platform the fork publishes no build for. The bundle
#     ships without that emulator, deliberately, and says so out loud
#   - whoever cuts a fork release moves the pin, in the same change as the
#     digests beside it in the catalogue

# QEMU carries our SX1262 device, its DIO1 line and the GPIO work upstream has
# not got, on every platform in the release: the fork's job cross-compiles the
# whole matrix on Linux runners, and refuses to publish at all if the SX1262
# device, its DIO1 line or the GPIO interrupt that drives it has gone missing.
# So the three bundles carry the same emulator rather than one of them carrying
# an older build that boots and then cannot relay.
#
# Built from 14b5e22f9a1ecf271940411859142fca1a88da25, the head of the fork's
# meshbench-main branch.
QEMU_RELEASE=v9.2.2-meshbench-sx1262-10
QEMU_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=qemu-xtensa-softmmu-v9.2.2_meshbench_sx1262_10-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.xz
QEMU_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=qemu-xtensa-softmmu-v9.2.2_meshbench_sx1262_10-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.xz
QEMU_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=qemu-xtensa-softmmu-v9.2.2_meshbench_sx1262_10-x86_64-w64-mingw32.tar.xz

# Renode carries the SEVONPEND fix, without which published nRF52 firmware
# sleeps for ever with its wake condition already true. This pin was not
# chosen: it is what happened to be publishable on the day the runtime fetcher
# landed, and it wants re-cutting from meshbench once the fix it is waiting on
# lands. Until then it is at least fixed, which is the part that stops a bundle
# changing underneath a release.
RENODE_RELEASE=meshbench-20260901-ca9f7e3
RENODE_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=renode-1.16.1.linux-portable-meshbench.tar.gz
RENODE_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=renode-meshbench-macos-arm64.tar.gz
RENODE_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=meshbench-renode-1.16.1.windows-portable.zip

# The SX1262 itself, which both emulators load and a native node links. Every
# emulated node needs it before either emulator, so a bundle without it ships
# two emulators nothing can drive.
#
# The tag tracks the ABI rather than the library: a host declares the version it
# needs, refuses one that cannot serve it, and the number in the tag is the one
# it is refusing against. This is ABI 1.3, the version with the byte-at-a-time
# SPI path, which is the only one an emulator can use.
CHIPMODEL_RELEASE=v1.3.0
CHIPMODEL_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=virtual-sx1262-linux-amd64.tar.gz
CHIPMODEL_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=virtual-sx1262-macos-arm64.tar.gz
CHIPMODEL_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=virtual-sx1262-windows-amd64.tar.gz
